# Seeding template — the default review panel

Read this only during seeding. It describes the panel spec-flow has always run, written out so a
repo can own it. It is a starting point, not a default: nothing in the pipeline reads this file,
and a repo that never writes its own gets no panel at all.

Copy the body below into the repo's `spec-flow/WORKFLOWS.md`, then edit it to what this repo
actually wants. If the repo already has a review culture — a lens it does not need, a threshold it
disagrees with — say that instead. An empty panel is a legitimate answer and is written out at the
bottom.

---

# Review policy

This repo owns this file. spec-flow reads it to decide who reviews an implementation and what has
to be true before the work leaves the pipeline. Edit it freely; nothing here is spec-flow's to
require.

## The panel

These lenses run **concurrently** on the branch diff, after implementation and before build:

- **`reviewer`** — does the code do what the approved spec says, and does it follow this repo's
  own documented conventions.
- **`code-reviewer`** — correctness only: logic errors, boundary and edge cases, unhandled error
  paths, concurrency and ordering, resource leaks.
- **`security-reviewer`** — input validation, injection, tenant isolation, authorization, secret
  and data exposure. It self-gates: on a diff touching no security-relevant surface it approves
  without findings.
- **`test-rigor-reviewer`** — whether the change's public surface and failure modes have tests
  worth their cost, in both directions: missing coverage and over-built tests.
- **`observability-reviewer`** — whether new code paths and failure modes are diagnosable in
  production: logging at the right level and without leaking secrets, metrics on error paths,
  nothing swallowed silently.

A lens may be any agent this repo can resolve, including one defined in `.claude/agents/`. A named
agent that cannot be resolved stops the run by name — it is never dropped or substituted.

## The gate

- **Must-fix** is every finding of severity `blocker` or `major`. `minor` and `nit` never block;
  they are reported to the owner on the pull request.
- The panel **approves** only when every lens reported, every lens approved, and must-fix is empty.
  A lens that returns no result is never counted as an approval.
- A lens that declines **without** naming a `blocker` or `major` earns a synthesized `major`
  finding, `unexplained-non-approval`. Declining is a real verdict and has to be justified.
- The panel runs at most **3 rounds**. The last round has no fix pass after it, so that is up to
  two rounds of fixes: review, fix, review, fix, review. If the panel has not approved by then, the
  pull request stays a draft, the outstanding findings go to the owner, and build and polish do not
  run.

## Exemptions

- A **content-only `type:docs`** change skips the panel entirely — a single doc-writing pass runs
  instead.
- A **`type:tech-debt`** change runs the full panel in behavior-preservation mode: there is no
  spec, so `reviewer` checks the diff against the issue's confirmed Direction and the existing test
  suite rather than against generated scenarios.

## If this repo wants no automated panel

Replace everything above with a statement that no panel runs and the owner's own review is the
gate. spec-flow will then go straight from implementation to build and polish, run no fix loop, and
say plainly on the pull request that no panel ran. It will never report an approval nobody gave.
